+## Configuration-once asset and escrow API
+
+Application code configures Torii, the chain, and its signer once. A batch call
+below submits one `TransferAssetBatch` instruction. In independent mode every
+valid leg commits and every rejected leg produces a correlated ledger event.
+
+```python
+from iroha_python import BatchMode, IrohaClient, Payment, Signer
+
+iroha = IrohaClient(
+    "http://127.0.0.1:8080",
+    "boi-poc",
+    Signer.ed25519("treasury@bank", TREASURY_PRIVATE_KEY),
+    fees="auto",
+)
+
+receipt = iroha.assets.transfer_batch(
+    "ds#is",
+    [
+        Payment(id="israel", to=ISRAEL, amount="20"),
+        Payment(id="roman", to=ROMAN, amount="60"),
+    ],
+    mode=BatchMode.INDEPENDENT,
+)
+print(receipt.hash)
+```
+
+`ds#is` is the on-chain asset alias applications should use. Before signing a
+native instruction, the SDK resolves it through Torii to the opaque canonical
+Base58 asset-definition ID. `Instruction.to_wire_json()` exposes the registered
+wire ID and concrete payload (including that resolved ID) for audit displays;
+`Instruction.to_json()` remains the canonical opaque signing representation.
+
+Conditional escrow definitions are first-class ledger data. Oracle claims are
+typed and ordered, the deadline is evaluated against ledger time, and the final
+passing attestation transfers custody automatically.
+
+```python
+from iroha_python import (
+    DeadlineCondition,
+    EscrowRelease,
+    OracleCondition,
+)
+
+iroha.escrows.open(
+    ESCROW_ID,
+    "ds#is",
+    "100",
+    LAPTOP_VENDOR,
+    [
+        OracleCondition.equals("processor", "i7", PRODUCT_ORACLE, order=1),
+        OracleCondition.at_most(
+            "delivery_days", 3, DELIVERY_ORACLE, order=2
+        ),
+        DeadlineCondition.within(days=7, id="within_7_days"),
+    ],
+    release=EscrowRelease.ALL_CONDITIONS,
+)
+
+product_oracle.escrows.attest(ESCROW_ID, "processor", "i7")
+delivery_oracle.escrows.attest(ESCROW_ID, "delivery_days", 3)
+state = iroha.escrows.get(ESCROW_ID)
+```
+
+BOI application policies that are deployed contracts use their real
+actor-specific entrypoints rather than fictional native instructions:
+
+```python
+receipt = iroha.contracts.call(
+    alias="wallet_registry::is",
+    entrypoint="record_wallet_consent",
+    arguments={
+        "consent_id": CONSENT_ID,
+        "subject": CUSTOMER,
+        "primary_psp": PSP,
+        "consent_digest": CONSENT_DIGEST,
+        "expires_at_ms": EXPIRES_AT_MS,
+    },
+)
+```
